Immutable polling wait operation

An immutable zero-page LDA followed by a same-page BPL/BEQ branch back to
that load owns complete six-cycle quiet iterations. BPL admits a clear sign
bit; BEQ admits a zero operand. Both use the same device deadline and ordinary
instruction boundary executor, including the real branch opcode and final
dummy read. No second interrupt, DMA or partial-instruction scheduler is added.
The existing BPL entry remains available to previously generated callers.

RAM observers, armed hooks at either instruction, modified program bytes,
isolated calls, tracing, pending interrupts and the existing mapper guard all
retain ordinary execution. Host counters count only actual fetch attempts;
collapsed service iterations are separate from instruction observations.

Service reporting starts on the first attempted operation, including operations
that execute only ordinary boundaries because device intervals admit no quiet
span. Attempts, entered operations and collapsed cycles are distinct measures.

The build opt-in defaults off. A netplay bootstrap may select the common
ordinary executor before frame zero; all wait entry points honor that fixed
policy. Shutdown restores the build's offline policy. Selection flushes clock
debt and adds no serialized machine state.
